WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.520
Jing Wang: So back to NGO 2.0
because we wanted to introduce

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:43.020
2.0 thinking and practice to
NGOs. Therefore, we started off

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.080
with a social media literacy
training.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:52.020
We teach grassroots NGOs and
western and central provinces of

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:57.030
China to learn how to use social
media to engage in participatory

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.680
thinking, launch interactive
advocacy campaigns, increase the

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:06.570
transparency of NGO operation,
design online crowdfunding

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.720
projects and learn Human
Centered Design to create

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.810
solutions to the problems that
NGOs are tackling.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:19.080
To date, we have trained
approximately 2200 NGOs. And

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.030
this is a part of our
curriculum. We also created

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.370
other programs, for example, we

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.960
developed a web 2.0 toolbox for
NGOs. We also build a

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.750
crowdsource philanthropy map,
over which more than 27,000 NGOs

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:44.850
have registered their project
data and organization data. We

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:49.110
also run civic hackathons in
collaboration with transnational

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:55.260
IT companies, domestic Chinese,
IT companies, software developer

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.130
and maker communities and
universities, especially

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:04.590
departments of computer science
and design. in universities, we

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:08.160
have worked with Ching Hua
University, and the University

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.000
of Science and Technology of
China and senior sign University

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:12.720
etc.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:03.000
Non confrontational activism is
a thematic thread running

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.700
through my new book, the other
digital China. Now, during my 10

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:14.970
year long experience of running
NGO 2.0, I encountered a variety

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.800
of puzzled responses from my
colleagues and friends in the

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.560
US, they ask, how could a
foreign NGO specializing ICT

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.150
activism survive at all in such
an adverse environment like

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:30.600
China?

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.660
This question, however, is a
false question. Because, first

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:41.910
of all, and to 2.0 is not an
international NGO. From day one,

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:47.730
I made sure that we set it up as
a local Chinese NGO operated by

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.350
a largely indigenous Chinese
team. We now have seven full

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:57.240
time employees in China. So the
challenges at the core of our

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.690
operation are not much different
from those faced by the other

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:05.130
grassroots NGOs in China. So we
should so the question we should

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:10.260
ask instead is, how have the
Chinese NGOs fared through the

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:16.080
success of reigns of an
authoritarian regime? Well, to

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.560
farewell in China requires a
different mindset and a

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.700
different strategy, which is
learning the art of restraint

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.240
and following the centuries old
cultural logic of finding the

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.270
middle ground whereby emissions
however difficult will get

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:35.100
accomplished. This for the
Chinese activists, this means

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.510
producing social good without
throwing a street revolution, or

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:42.780
confronting the state openly and
aggressively.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:49.260
During the past 10 years, I was
exposed bit by a bit to a

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:53.790
gradually unfolding captivating
picture of social media activism

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:57.390
in China, in which multiple
players from diverse sectors are

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:02.160
leveraging the network effect of
2.0 to equate incremental change

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:06.600
in China. And this book is
devoted to the ICT practices

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.250
emerging from China's social
sector caught at a specific,

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.360
particular historical moment,
thanks partly to the arrival of

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.740
2.0 technology, and cyber
utopianism, and partly to the

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:24.330
Communist Party's alleged
commitment to policies and

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:29.010
energizing the weak social
sector. Now with this book, I

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:34.140
was trying to answer to ask the
question, what is the ecosystem

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:41.040
of social media activism looking
like in China today in the old

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:46.650
media environment of the major
civic actors for NGOs, but since

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:52.650
2009, after social media had
gone mainstream in China, Mike

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:57.600
did this old ecosystem has
changed. Because microblogging

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.620
brought in four new groups of
social actors, they are the free

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.400
agents, corporate sector,
software, developer communities

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.350
and maker labs and the
university sector, and NGO 2.0

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.980
designed projects that involve
participants from all those

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:18.480
sectors. I should also note that
the great majority of social

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.190
actors we collaborate with are
Chinese millennials and members

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:28.560
of the generation seeing all
those social actors share a

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.060
commitment to making incremental
change rather than throwing a

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.440
street revolution.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.190
And together,

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:42.440
those actors from diverse
sectors are building an

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:46.250
invisible coalition to bring
incremental change to Chinese

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:51.440
society in spite of censorship.
If you want me to choose a

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:55.910
single sentence to describe this
book, it would be this is a book

00:06:55.910 --> 00:07:00.860
about the gray zones in China,
integrate zones Chinese

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:05.180
activists practice non
contentious social actions. So

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.450
let's take a deeper dive into
this keyword, non

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:14.480
confrontational activism and and
ask what kind of politics it

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:18.860
implies. And how does the
concept and practice of non

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:23.000
confrontational ism challenged
the mainstream Western liberal

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:28.190
thinking about activism? Chinese
social actors I named above

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:32.660
don't fit squarely into the
profile of activists prescribed

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:36.080
in the western liberal
tradition, because Chinese

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.280
changemakers are walking around
obstacles rather than breaking

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:46.160
through them. And they navigate
tactfully between what is lawful

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.420
and what is illegitimate. We're
all familiar with a Western

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:54.380
paradigm that tend to equate
action with resistance and

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:58.580
social change with street
revolutions. And I think it is

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:02.870
time that non confrontational
activism is conceptualized,

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:07.670
fully documented with case
studies so that we can put on

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:12.200
the front burner, a very
important question, which is the

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:17.930
agency of activists operating in
authoritarian countries. Now

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:21.740
generally speaking, activists
practicing non confrontational

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:26.690
ism are anonymous because their
actions purposefully attract no

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:29.780
attention. They stay on the
margins of history, and they

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:35.210
remain peripheral to academic
discussions about social

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:39.500
actions, even though it prevails
in all autocratic societies,

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:45.140
where activists resort to other
means of serving social good,

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:51.230
then openly rebelling and openly
critiquing. In my book, I

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:54.830
incorporated a chapter on the
critical literature revolving

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:58.100
around the concept of practice
of non confrontational this.

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:03.830
Here, I want to single out one
such book James C. Scott's

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:07.820
Weapons of the Week: Everyday
Forms of Personal Resistance.

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:11.540
James guard introduced the
concept of invisible agents and

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:15.380
their quiet and piecemeal
tactics. He also gave credit to

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:19.010
what he called the practice of
calculated conformity.

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:25.030
He did well, James Scott's
peasants typically avoid a

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.580
direct and dramatic
confrontation with the

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:32.020
authorities. But instead of
condemning the peasant silence,

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:37.840
as complicitous, or devoid of
politics, Scott locates the

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:43.240
sights of peasant action in
micro and conspicuous everyday

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:47.290
forms of food tracking, false
compliance, feigned ignorance

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:52.210
and so on. And this book
represents a significant

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:57.490
milestone in valorizing the
powerless as political agents.

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:02.530
Now when we turn to a country
like China or other illiberal

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:07.780
societies, where open resistance
is an exception rather than the

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:12.970
norm. We, as researchers are
called upon to go beyond the

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:18.430
dichotomous mode of thinking to
solve a puzzle. The puzzle is

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:22.090
why are they exploited in those
countries accept their situation

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:25.720
as a normal, or even as a
justifiable part of social

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:29.380
order? Are Chinese people
fatalistic, complicitous or

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:33.220
paralyzed by fear and cowardice?
So this question has been

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:37.630
sitting on a lot of people's
mind. Surely, if the Chinese

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:43.690
government could hire as many as
2 million people to insert

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:48.190
deceptive writings into social
media posts, shouldn't we have

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:51.160
good reasons to believe that
Chinese censorship has

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.780
penetrated every corner of that
society, and that the censors

00:10:55.780 --> 00:11:00.400
have manipulated the public
opinions effortlessly? Well,

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:04.300
that is the conventional reading
of the muted consensus of

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:08.260
Chinese people over maintaining
the status quo. And that

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:12.910
formulation relegates the entire
population of China into the

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:18.040
category of the brainwashed. In
reality, though, we know that

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.250
Chinese people have more choices
than being brainwashed and

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:25.960
becoming martyrs. So what is
missing? In the scholarly

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:30.520
research on China is the massive
middle ground in which

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:34.540
conformity is often a self
conscious strategy, and that it

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:38.740
might be possible to think of a
continuum of situations ranging

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:42.430
from the free dialogue, what
carbon was called ideal speech

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:46.570
situation, or the public sphere,
all the way to the concentration

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:50.830
camp. So what is understudied in
the China field is that

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:55.090
continuum of situations, or the
middle ground, or the gray

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:58.990
zones, in which the Chinese
activists navigate day in and

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:03.490
day out quite successfully. As
we all knew that the favorite

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:07.840
topic, the hottest topic for
digital China's scholars in the

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:13.000
West is Chinese censorship, with
this book and taking a different

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:18.550
path. If we want to understand
the real everyday China, it is

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:22.930
imperative we go beyond the
simple dichotomy of white and

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:28.420
black and turn our gaze toward
the gray. So all this may sound

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:32.080
a bit abstract. What did I mean
when I say Chinese activists

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:36.670
operate in the gray zones? So
let's take NGOs for example, and

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:41.620
illustrate how NGOs in China
function in a tightly controlled

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:45.730
social space? Or we could ask a
slightly different question.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:50.140
What is the relationship between
Chinese NGOs and Chinese state?

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:55.720
The shorthand answer to that
question is NGOs in China are

00:12:55.720 --> 00:13:00.700
compelled obliged to learn how
to navigate within the state

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:05.800
apparatus. Now, if you want me
to choose three adjectives to

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:11.230
describe Chinese NGOs, they
would be semi official semi

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:15.400
popular semi autonomous, semi
official refer to the NGOs

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:18.790
navigating within the state
structure, somehow popular

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:22.150
refers to the self
identification of grassroots

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:26.740
NGOs. Semi autonomy is the
concept I will explain later.

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:34.660
There is a Chinese saying, don't
start, hey, if you're going to

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:38.500
learn quite a few Chinese
sayings tonight, the most

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:43.570
invisible place is the spot
right underneath the light this

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:49.150
the spot the spot, which means
no place is safer than the place

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:53.140
of danger. In other words, under
the surveillance of the party

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:56.830
state, it is easier to carve out
breathing spaces within the

00:13:56.830 --> 00:14:01.390
state structure within the plant
space, then create an outside.

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:09.990
So here, hence the Chinese
paradox. The closer the

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:13.680
relationship of NGO is to the
Chinese state, the more

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:14.640
autonomous,

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:20.250
the more autonomous, it would
become. Now, this may still

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:23.640
sound abstract, so let me share
with you an anecdote.

00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:30.080
In the late 1990s, I started a
collaborative research project

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:35.720
with a professor at Peking
University on popular media and

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:40.250
popular culture in China. We
wanted to hold an international

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:44.690
conference in Beijing. But
finding a safe conference venue

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:48.650
was very difficult because the
word media in the conference

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:54.470
title is politically sensitive
in China. We eventually overcame

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:59.030
the problem by holding our
conference at a state owned and

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:03.740
state run hotel, we were able to
do this because one of our

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:07.130
participants had a personal
relationship with a hotel

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:10.730
manager. So there was no
surveillance, no questions

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:15.530
asked, and the conference went
on very smoothly. So this is a

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:19.430
very good example that
illustrates what I meant by the

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:23.810
safest place is the place of
danger. The most invisible place

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:29.210
is the spot right underneath the
light. So those habitually cling

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:32.870
on to the Western binary
thinking and Western binary

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:38.330
dichotomous paradigms will have
difficulties grasping how

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:43.550
Chinese people navigate in the
seemingly seamless web of

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:48.350
political control. Now, I want
to emphasize that all Chinese

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:52.490
people, not just Chinese
activists have a half a circle

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:57.260
mindset. They are very good at
finding creative ways of walking

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:03.470
around obstacles. That's the set
of NGOs in a liberal societies

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:07.100
tend to practice non
confrontational activism by

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:11.240
default, and it is imperative
they make change within the

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:16.250
system. I'm going to give you a
quote, to further unbundle this

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:22.190
paradox. This is this quote was
from Anthony sage, he was the

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:27.380
former chief of Ford Foundation
in Beijing. He now teaches at

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:32.600
Harvard's Kennedy School. So he
says Chinese NGOs voluntary

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:36.020
subordination to the existing
state structure should be viewed

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:40.190
not as a measure of expediency
but a strategic move to enhance

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:43.520
the ability to manipulate the
official and semi official

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:48.080
institutions for their own
advantage, which means making

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:50.990
more impact on society and
gaining a louder voice in

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:54.770
Policymaking discussion stand if
they were to remain completely

00:16:54.770 --> 00:17:01.580
autonomous. And I may add a risk
being crushed immediately by the

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:05.360
authorities if they create the
NGO outside of the state

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:13.580
structure. So fearing lies the
fundamental difference between

00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:18.200
Western NGOs and their Chinese
counterpart, I want to take this

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:22.880
opportunity to point out another
level another set of difference

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:27.710
between Western civil society
and Chinese Civil Society, what

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:31.520
sense civil society came into
existence as a result of the

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:35.840
agitating growth of the social,
the Chinese Civil society could

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:40.130
only be born and flourished as a
result of the voluntary retreat

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:44.630
of the state home society. And
that retreat of the state from

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:49.640
society, of course, has been a
gradual and measured process.

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:55.700
There is no doubt that the gray
zones in China were much bigger

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:59.690
in size under the hooting Tao
region than under the current

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:03.650
region. However, I would say
there will always be a gray zone

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:07.940
for Chinese activists, you will
never disappear when a

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:13.010
politically sensitive issue
areas occur that may endanger an

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:17.480
NGO, the NGOs well and its
ability to negotiate with the

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:22.790
local state out of the out of
the dilemma is critical to its

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:28.400
survival. In the q&amp;a, I would be
glad to share with you more

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:32.150
examples or more strategies of
how to cope with that the

00:18:32.150 --> 00:18:38.270
sensors so bearing
nonconfrontational ethos in

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:42.290
mind, let's move on to the
examples of social media

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:48.140
activism staged Weibo. First, a
quick definition of social media

00:18:48.140 --> 00:18:51.920
activism. It refers to social
actions triggered through peer

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:54.980
to peer networking between weak
ties and furthermore, those

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:58.790
social actions have mobilized
the viral communications to

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:02.960
create online support
communities at scale, so I

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:05.600
picked out five examples.

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:13.100
The first example is called a
shaved head action. In the mid

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:18.170
2000s Guangzhou based activists
Anil Hwei wrote a web blog,

00:19:18.350 --> 00:19:24.650
calling for 1000 netizens fellow
netizens to shave their heads as

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:29.330
a symbolic gesture to stop the
city's night illumination

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:34.280
project on the program which
would cost the city taxpayers

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:38.570
more than 150 million yen
without much justification.

00:19:39.410 --> 00:19:45.560
pamunkey posted his photos
before and after the shave, and

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:51.170
on Weibo, and in 20 hours, he
attracted 4000 retweets and

00:19:51.170 --> 00:19:56.000
recruited more than 20 people to
follow him and shaved their

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:01.100
heads, including a young woman
and a few children. So what does

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:05.390
shading one's head have anything
to do with energy conservation?

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:11.150
Well, Anil Hwei argued mockingly
that 1000 shaved heads could

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:15.710
generate enough brightness to
light up the Pearl River and

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:21.050
